The bug: `len = strncpy((char *)pwd, (const char *)upwd, sizeof(password_t) - 1);` — `strncpy` returns a pointer to `pwd` (a stack buffer), and this pointer value is assigned to `len` (a `size_t`). So `len` becomes a huge value (a stack address).

Then `stun_check_message_integrity_by_key_str(ct, buf, len, key, pwd, shatype)` is called with this huge `len`. Inside:

```c
stun_attr_ref sar = stun_attr_get_first_by_type_str(buf, len, STUN_ATTRIBUTE_MESSAGE_INTEGRITY);
```

This calls `stun_attr_get_first_str(buf, len)` → `stun_get_command_message_len_str(buf, len)`:

```c
int stun_get_command_message_len_str(const uint8_t *buf, size_t len) {
  if (len < STUN_HEADER_LENGTH)
    return -1;
  size_t bufLen = (size_t)(nswap16(((const uint16_t *)(buf))[1]) + STUN_HEADER_LENGTH);
  if (bufLen > len) {
    return -1;
  }
  return bufLen;
}
```

Since `len` is now a huge stack pointer value, the check `bufLen > len` will essentially never trigger (bufLen max is 65555). So the header's claimed length is trusted.

Then `stun_attr_get_first_str` returns an attribute pointer within the claimed length. Since the claimed length can be up to 65555 but the actual buffer is only `Size` bytes (up to 5120), the attribute pointer can point beyond the actual buffer.

Then in `stun_check_message_integrity_by_key_str`:
```c
